1. Scope and prior rights

This license governs HyperL material its rights holder first distributes under
this license, including authorized later changes ("Covered Software"). It grants
only rights the Licensor owns or is authorized to grant. Separately identified
third-party licenses and earlier grants are not replaced.

HyperL through commit 67b30e09741f4bba1672a891644fc73abb1f9855, including released
versions through v0.1.0-alpha.5, was offered under Apache License 2.0. Those grants
remain available for that material, including continued enterprise use and
permitted forks/modifications. Unchanged material from that history does not lose
its Apache rights in a later distribution. Only newly covered rights, not rights
already granted under Apache or another license, depend on this license. The
Apache text and scope history accompany this license.

2. Definitions

"You" means an individual or the legal entity on whose behalf software is used.
"Group" includes that entity and entities controlling, controlled by or under
common control with it, directly or indirectly. Control means power to direct
management/policies or ownership of more than 50 percent of voting interests.
Unrelated customers, suppliers or research collaborators are not affiliates
merely because they work together.

"Large Entity" means a Group meeting EITHER threshold: (a) consolidated annual
gross revenue of at least US$10,000,000 in its most recently completed fiscal
year, or an approved annual operating budget of at least US$10,000,000 where
revenue is not an appropriate measure; or (b) 100 or more employees across the
Group, including full-time and part-time employees. Intragroup revenue is not
counted twice. Non-US amounts use a publicly quoted exchange rate at fiscal
year end or, for a budget, its approval date. An entity without a completed
fiscal year uses its approved current annual operating budget. Keep a reasonable
written basis for classification; no personal employee data reporting is required.

"Small Business" means a business whose Group is below BOTH thresholds. A
subsidiary, contractor, service intermediary or legal form does not turn a Large
Entity's own deployment into a Small Business deployment.

"Developer Use" means creating, studying, modifying, building, testing, debugging,
benchmarking, demonstrating or contributing software in a non-production
development environment. It includes commercial application development and
developers employed by Large Entities.

"Research Use" means academic, educational or scientific investigation,
experimentation, reproducibility, teaching or publication, including
non-production industrial research. Funding or employment alone does not
disqualify research. Routine operations or production service are not research
merely because they are called a pilot, experiment or research project.

"Production Use" means operating Covered Software for ongoing business or
organizational operations, delivering a service, or embedding/deploying it in
a product used operationally by You or Your customers. Genuine Developer Use,
Research Use and individual personal use are excluded. A Large Entity's
operational deployment remains its use when administered by an affiliate or
contractor.

3. Free permissions

Subject to the conditions below, You may use, reproduce, study, modify and
distribute Covered Software worldwide without a license fee for:
  a. individual personal use;
  b. Developer Use, without an organization-size limit;
  c. Research Use, without an organization-size limit; and
  d. Production Use by a Small Business or other entity below both thresholds.

These permissions do not expire after six months. A developer's personal license
does not authorize otherwise restricted Large Entity Production Use. Merely
receiving independent results from a service is not itself using Covered Software.

4. Six-month Large Entity production trial

A Large Entity may make Production Use without a fee for one trial of six
consecutive calendar months. It starts on the date its Group first makes
Production Use requiring this license while meeting a Large Entity threshold.
Use of independently Apache-licensed material does not start a trial. If a
Small Business grows into a Large Entity, its trial starts when it first
continues such Production Use after meeting a threshold; there are no retroactive
fees for earlier eligible Small Business use.

The trial ends at the start of the same numbered date six months later, or
the final day of that month if the numbered date does not exist. Pausing,
reinstalling, upgrading, changing hosts, creating an affiliate, renaming the
organization or using a derivative does not restart or extend it. Combining
Groups does not restart a trial already used by the deployment's operator.
Record the dates internally; no telemetry, activation server or upload is required.

To continue Large Entity Production Use after the trial, obtain a separate paid
commercial license expressly accepted in writing by the Licensor and You before
expiry. Prices, covered entities, duration, deployment scope, support, taxes
and payment terms must be agreed in that document. This license creates no
automatic subscription, invoice, retroactive fee or promised price. An enquiry,
pending negotiation or self-assessed payment is not a commercial license.

Without an effective agreement, stop the affected Production Use when the trial
expires. Free eligible uses and independent Apache/third-party rights remain.
You need not delete Your own inputs or independent outputs.

5. Redistribution and derivatives

When distributing Covered Software or derivatives containing it, retain this
license, applicable copyright/attribution notices, NOTICE, Apache history and
third-party notices; clearly identify modifications. Source/binary distribution
is permitted with or without a fee under these conditions. Inform recipients
of the same use conditions for Covered Software. You cannot grant broader
rights to it, remove the enterprise condition, or grant a new trial to a Group
that has already used its trial. Recipients receive rights from the applicable
rights holder, not an assertion that You own their copyright.

Independent software merely calling or linking to HyperL may use its own
license. Recipients still need applicable rights for Covered Software they
actually deploy. This license requires no disclosure of independent application
source, user data, model weights or outputs. Outputs without copied Covered
Software are not licensed here. Model, dataset, content and other rights remain
separate.

